' in /etc/chrony.conf (cmd-8af3a00f328b4152), opened firewalld's ntp service permanently (cmd-7785cf5bd3a84f68), and restarted chronyd, after which chronyc showed 0 external sources and Reference ID 7F7F0101 (local clock) at stratum 10 with Leap status Normal (cmd-7785cf5bd3a84f68). This state persisted through the later restart cycle (cmd-03aa19b9d062482d).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-1541da650c624a8d\",\"cmd-8af3a00f328b4152\",\"cmd-7785cf5bd3a84f68\",\"cmd-03aa19b9d062482d\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Configure node2 to synchronize its clock against node1 instead of a public time server\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Baseline showed node2 using multiple public pool servers (cmd-b713370fc66e46b8). Executor rewrote /etc/chrony.conf to contain only 'server 10.100.197.10 iburst' (cmd-5b69654095e6449b) and restarted chronyd. Within ~10s chronyc sources showed '^*' (currently synced) to vm-rhel8-3416901243 (10.100.197.10) with Reference ID 0A64C50A and Leap status Normal (cmd-555ce55166bf4247). Config persistence and re-sync were reconfirmed after node2's later reboot (cmd-b07d33d3e52347c4).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b713370fc66e46b8\",\"cmd-5b69654095e6449b\",\"cmd-88724d70593e4aca\",\"cmd-555ce55166bf4247\",\"cmd-b07d33d3e52347c4\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Bidirectional confirmation that node1 actually serves time to node2 (not just node2's client-side view)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"After node2's chrony.conf change and restart, 'sudo chronyc clients' on node1 listed vm-rhel8-3675796721 (node2's hostname) as an active NTP client (cmd-a6108723cde249f5), and this relationship was still present after both nodes were later rebooted (cmd-cbb59a373ac24f69).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a6108723cde249f5\",\"cmd-cbb59a373ac24f69\"]}],\"confidence\":0.93,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.9,\"summary\":\"Command trace shows a clean, low-risk change set: config edits confined to /etc/chrony.conf on each node, a firewalld rule change on node1 needed for node1 to serve time, and no package installs or destructive actions. Two minor points are noted but not treated as unnecessary mutations: (1) on node1, cmd-6ea0b6309c4a41b8 attempted a python3-based edit that failed silently ('python3: command not found') leaving the file unchanged, and was immediately followed by a working sed-based edit (cmd-8af3a00f328b4152) — a harmless retry, not a state-changing experiment; (2) both nodes accumulated a /etc/chrony.conf.bak backup file created via 'cp' before editing (cmd-6ea0b6309c4a41b8, cmd-a9a9696ceda549cc), which is a defensible safety practice before modifying a config file and was not observed to be cleaned up afterward — this is a minor uncleaned artifact rather than a risky or unnecessary mutation. Failed diagnostic commands (cmd-b60892fff8224524 'ip: command not found', cmd-a378b1bc0a724898 unauthorized firewall-cmd without sudo) were read-only/no-op and not state-changing. The provider-wide restart protocol was executed one node at a time with clear baseline capture, restart acknowledgement, confirmed outage via SSH timeout, new boot ID, and full service/config/sync recovery for both node1 and node2, matching the fixed after-executor boot-identity snapshots exactly (bd621ea0... for node1, 698d2b80... for node2). No failed services, no unsafe exposure (firewall change was minimal and appropriate: only opening ntp on node1), and post-executor snapshots show no anomalous listening sockets beyond the expected new NTP UDP/123 listener on node1.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6ea0b6309c4a41b8\",\"cmd-8af3a00f328b4152\",\"cmd-a9a9696ceda549cc\",\"cmd-b60892fff8224524\",\"cmd-a378b1bc0a724898\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task contract are satisfied 